During the checkpoint-trailer writer/reader mismatch investigation (Sept 2026, entireio/cli branch `fix-checkpoint-trailer-block`), the user interrupted mid-validation: "you are not supposed to fix the write mismatch bug in this branch. simply confirm the bug and possible solution. i can create a pr for that" — even though the written task brief said to implement the smallest fix after confirming.

Refined Sept 2026 on branch `refactor-git-metadata-owner` (PR #2241). The user said "commit the changes", then interrupted the commit with "do not commit on your behalf". After I stopped, reported the working-tree state, and listed the exact commit messages and file grouping I intended, they said "commit the changes" again and the commits were welcome.

**Why:** The boundary is not committing itself, it is committing something they have not seen. They want the split, the messages, and any open questions in front of them first.

**How to apply:** Do the edits, run fmt/lint/tests, then show the proposed commit split and messages and stop. Commit on the explicit go-ahead that follows. Same for PR descriptions: draft, show, publish when told ("update the pr description" was welcomed once the text had been reviewed). Surface conflicts rather than resolving them silently — the `commit` skill forbids Co-Authored-By trailers while the session harness requires them; I flagged it, they did not object, and the harness trailers went in.
---
